  const requests = urls.map(async (url) => {
    try {
      const response = await fetch(url);
      return response.status;
    } catch (error) {
      // fetch() only throws on network errors (e.g., DNS failure, no internet, CORS)
      // HTTP errors like 404 or 500 will still return a valid status code above.
      return null; // Or return error.message if you prefer
    }
  });

  return Promise.all(requests);
}

// Example usage:
const urls = [
  'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/posts/1', // 200
  'https://jsonplaceholder.typicode.com/404-page', // 404
  'https://this-domain-does-not-exist.invalid'     // null (network error)
];

getUrlStatuses(urls).then((statuses) => {
  console.log(statuses); // Output: [200, 404, null]
});
```

### Key Details:
* **Order is preserved:** Even if the second URL responds faster than the first, `Promise.all` ensures the output array aligns with the input `urls` array.
* **Error Handling:** Wrapping each `fetch` in a `try/catch` block prevents a single network failure from rejecting the entire `Promise.all` call.
* **Environment:** This works natively in all modern browsers and in Node.js (v18+).

