# Fetching URL Statuses in Order

Here's a clean implementation that fetches all URLs concurrently while preserving the original order:

```javascript
/**
 * Fetches the HTTP status code for each URL.
 * @param {string[]} urls - List of URLs to check.
 * @returns {Promise<Array<{url: string, status: number|null, error: string|null}>>}
 */
async function getStatuses(urls) {
  const results = await Promise.all(
    urls.map(async (url) => {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(url, { method: 'HEAD' });
        return { url, status: response.status, error: null };
      } catch (err) {
        return { url, status: null, error: err.message };
      }
    })
  );
  return results;
}
```

## Usage

```javascript
const urls = [
  'https://example.com',
  'https://httpstat.us/404',
  'https://this-domain-does-not-exist-xyz.com',
];

const statuses = await getStatuses(urls);
console.log(statuses);
// [
//   { url: 'https://example.com', status: 200, error: null },
//   { url: 'https://httpstat.us/404', status: 404, error: null },
//   { url: 'https://this-domain-does-not-exist-xyz.com', status: null, error: 'fetch failed' },
// ]
