## Notes

- **Order is guaranteed** by writing into `results[index]` (the pool version) or by `Promise.all` (the simple version) — never by `forEach` with async callbacks.
- **Failures vs. HTTP errors:** a `404`/`500` is a *successful* request with an error status, so it comes back as `404`/`500`. A network failure has no status, so it's `null`. If you'd rather distinguish them, return `{ status, ok, error }` objects instead.
- **`GET` vs `HEAD`:** `HEAD` avoids downloading bodies but some servers/CDNs respond `405` to it. If you use `HEAD`, treat `405` as "fall back to `GET`".
- **Node.js:** global `fetch` requires Node 18+. On older versions use `node-fetch` (v3 is ESM) or the built-in `https` module.
- **Rate limiting:** if a site blocks you, lower `concurrency` and add a delay between requests.

```js
/**
 * Fetch each URL and return its HTTP response status, in the same order as the input.
 *
 * @param {string[]} urls        - URLs to request
 * @param {object}   [options]
 * @param {number}   [options.timeout=10000] - Per-request timeout in ms
 * @param {object}   [options.headers={}]    - Headers sent with each request
 * @returns {Promise<number[]>} - Status codes (0 for network errors/timeouts)
 */
async function getResponseStatuses(urls, options = {}) {
  const { timeout = 10_000, headers = {} } = options;

  return Promise.all(
    urls.map(async (url) => {
      const controller = new AbortController();
      const timer = setTimeout(() => controller.abort(), timeout);

      try {
        const response = await fetch(url, {
          method: 'GET',
          headers,
          signal: controller.signal,
          redirect: 'follow',
        });
        return response.status;
      } catch {
        // Network failure, invalid URL, DNS error, or timeout
        return 0;
      } finally {
        clearTimeout(timer);
      }
    })
  );
}
```

**Example:**

```js
const urls = [
  'https://example.com',
  'https://httpbin.org/status/404',
  'https://httpbin.org/status/500',
];

const statuses = await getResponseStatuses(urls);
console.log(statuses); // [200, 404, 500]
```

**Notes**

- `Promise.all` preserves the input order even though the requests run concurrently and finish out of order.
- `fetch` only rejects on network-level failures, not on HTTP error codes, so `404`/`500` are returned as-is while unreachable hosts yield `0`.
- If you'd rather see error details than `0`, replace the `catch` block with:

```js
} catch (err) {
  return { status: 0, error: err.name === 'AbortError' ? 'timeout' : err.message };
}
```

```js
/**
 * Fetches each URL and returns its HTTP status code, in the same order as the input.
 * Network failures yield `null` instead of aborting the whole batch.
 *
 * @param {string[]} urls
 * @returns {Promise<(number|null)[]>}
 */
async function getResponseStatuses(urls) {
  return Promise.all(
    urls.map(async (url) => {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(url);
        return response.status;
      } catch {
        return null; // network error / invalid URL
      }
    })
  );
}
```

**Example:**

```js
const urls = [
  'https://example.com',
  'https://httpbin.org/status/404',
  'https://httpbin.org/status/500',
];

console.log(await getResponseStatuses(urls)); // [200, 404, 500]
```

**Notes:**
- `Promise.all` preserves the order of the input array, so results line up with `urls` regardless of which request finishes first. All requests run concurrently.
- `fetch` only rejects on network-level failures; HTTP error statuses (4xx/5xx) resolve normally, so they are returned as-is.
- If you'd rather fail fast on network errors, drop the `try/catch` — `Promise.all` will then reject with the first error.
- Redirects are followed by default, so the returned status is the final response's. Use `fetch(url, { redirect: 'manual' })` to see the original 3xx status instead.
- Requires Node.js 18+ or a browser environment.

```javascript
/**
 * Fetches each URL and returns its HTTP status code, in the original order.
 *
 * @param {string[]} urls - The URLs to request.
 * @returns {Promise<(number|null)[]>} - Status codes in the same order as `urls`.
 *   `null` is used for URLs that could not be reached (network error, invalid URL).
 */
async function getResponseStatuses(urls) {
  return Promise.all(
    urls.map(async (url) => {
      try {
        const response = await fetch(url, { redirect: 'follow' });
        return response.status;
      } catch {
        return null; // Network failure, DNS error, invalid URL, etc.
      }
    })
  );
}

// Example usage:
const statuses = await getResponseStatuses([
  'https://example.com',
  'https://httpbin.org/status/404',
  'https://httpbin.org/status/500',
]);

console.log(statuses); // e.g. [200, 404, 500]
```

**Notes:**

- `Promise.all` preserves order regardless of which request finishes first, since each mapped promise resolves with the status of its own URL.
- `fetch` only rejects on network-level failures; HTTP error responses (4xx, 5xx) resolve normally, so their status codes are returned as-is.
- If you'd rather fail loudly on unreachable URLs, replace the `catch` block with `throw` — but then one bad URL rejects the whole call. Alternatively, use `Promise.allSettled` and inspect each result:

```javascript
async function getResponseStatusesStrict(urls) {
  const results = await Promise.allSettled(
    urls.map((url) => fetch(url).then((r) => r.status))
  );
  return results.map((r) =>
    r.status === 'fulfilled' ? r.value : null
  );
}
```

- In Node.js (v18+), `fetch` is built in; on older versions install `node-fetch`. In browsers, be aware of CORS restrictions — cross-origin requests may fail without proper CORS headers on the server.
